 \section{Conclusion}
-Reference-preserving speculative execution requires a qualified transaction, not merely correct acceptance logic at one decision. The studied RTX implementation constrains attention query layout during target prefill and continuation while retaining other batched computation. Natural crosses and a four-switch qualification study support that finite repair scope; complete responses and width failures define its boundary. Paid ordinary and matched comparisons then separate the deployment gain from the speculative increment. The resulting decisions are mixed: the frozen RTX anchor helps on aggregate, a wider qualified draft loses, the B570 repair remains slower than strong baselines, and M4 and 358H restrict the case for added control and draft-only optimization. The study supplies concrete implementation evidence and reproducible cost comparisons within one model family, while preserving failures that prevent broader claims.
+A runtime that must reproduce an ordinary greedy output needs to preserve the stateful transaction as well as its acceptance rule. In the tested RTX implementation, a query-layout change during target prefill and continuation retains other batched work and qualifies a finite full-response panel. Natural crosses motivate that scope, while the complete phase matrix and wider-geometry failures bound it.
+
+Paid ordinary and matched comparisons then change the performance interpretation: $1.742\times$ RTX request speed includes a $1.683\times$ draft-free target-path gain, leaving $1.035\times$ from speculation on that path. The increment is uneven and reverses in one round. A wider qualified draft loses, B570 endpoint repair remains slower than strong baselines, and the separate M4/358H cases constrain the value of added control and draft-only optimization. These outcomes support a concrete design practice within the studied family: diagnose local dependence, qualify the complete output under an explicit configuration, then charge both baselines before selecting speculation.
